\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces The ``Reliability Cliff'' in multi-tenant XAI. Spearman rank agreement of per-tenant SHAP rankings against the data-rich reference (Tenant~A) plotted against tenant size $n_t$ on a logarithmic axis. Agreement collapses below the conventional $\rho = 0.70$ reliability threshold at the $n = 1{,}000$ tier (Tenant~C), marking the operational cold-start boundary used by the Maturity-Aware Routing gate. Values plotted are the leakage-corrected empirical figures from Chapter~\ref {ch:results}.}}{3}{figure.caption.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Mechanistic comparison of SHAP and LIME. SHAP (left) decomposes the model output $f(x)$ as the sum of the expected output $E[f(x)]$ and the per-feature Shapley contributions $\phi _i$; LIME (right) fits a locally weighted linear surrogate $g$ to perturbed samples drawn around the instance $x$ within the locality kernel $\pi _x$.}}{11}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Conceptual \emph {Reliability Cliff} relating tenant data volume $n$ (log scale) to explanation fidelity (Spearman $\rho $ against the data-rich baseline). The horizontal dashed line marks the $\rho = 0.70$ reliability bound; the vertical dotted line marks the hypothesised cold-start threshold at $n = 1{,}000$.}}{14}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Research conceptual framework: from multi-tenant isolation to the Maturity-Aware AI Routing decision gate. The cold-start branch is taken inclusively at $n_t \le 1{,}000$ (equivalently, the code-side guard $n_t < \textsf {MIN\_LOCAL\_EXPLANATION\_RECORDS}$ with the constant set to $1{,}001$).}}{16}{figure.caption.6}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Stratified slicing of the base Olist dataset ($N = 96{,}460$) into a shared $20\%$ global hold-out evaluation partition and five disjoint simulated tenant tiers ($n = 50{,}000$; $5{,}000$; $1{,}000$; $200$; $50$), with class prevalence preserved at each tier.}}{24}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Maturity-Aware AI Routing architectural gate. The \texttt {LatePaymentPredictor.predict\_or\_heuristic()} method inspects the tenant's invoice count $n_t$ against the inclusive boundary $n_t \le \texttt {COLD\_START\_INVOICE\_THRESHOLD} = 1{,}000$ (equivalently, $n_t < \textsf {MIN\_LOCAL\_EXPLANATION\_RECORDS} = 1{,}001$). Cold-start tenants are dispatched to the global heuristic served from \texttt {cold\_start\_heuristic.json}; data-mature tenants are dispatched to the per-tenant SHAP/LIME pipeline. Both branches emit a single schema-conformant response payload.}}{28}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Predictive performance across simulated tenant tiers under the leakage-free feature regime. AUC-ROC is uniformly modest in tiers~A--D ($\approx 0.61$--$0.66$) and rises anomalously to $0.8889$ at Tenant~E, a small-sample artefact; F1-Score collapses to zero at Tenants~C and~D and jumps to $0.6667$ at Tenant~E. Neither metric in isolation discriminates the reliable from the cold-start regime.}}{37}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Joint degradation of SHAP rank fidelity (Spearman $\rho $ against the Tenant~A baseline; left axis) and SHAP attribution concentration (absolute variance; right axis, log scale) across tenant data volumes under the leakage-free regime. The $\rho = 0.70$ reliability bound is crossed for the first time at the $n = 1{,}000$ Reliability Cliff, and SHAP variance rises by more than two orders of magnitude from baseline to the cold-start tier.}}{40}{figure.caption.12}%
